  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how artificial light at night is emitted, redirected, and measured across a landscape. It links local lighting near buildings and trees with broader skyglow seen from the ground and from space. Several pathways are shown: some light goes directly upward, some travels horizontally, some is reflected from the ground or nearby surfaces, and some is blocked or reduced by shielding and by vegetation. Once in the atmosphere, light is scattered by air and by clouds, which spreads illumination over larger distances and contributes to the glow of the night sky.\n\nThe diagram also emphasizes that different observation methods capture different parts of this system. A satellite detects mainly upward-directed light leaving the surface, while direct field measurements near buildings and trees represent the actual exposure experienced by animals at ground level. An all-sky camera records the resulting skyglow after atmospheric and cloud scattering. The scene makes clear that exposure at ecological scales depends not just on lamp brightness, but also on geometry, reflection, shading, and atmospheric conditions. Overall, the image presents nighttime light pollution as a multi-step process that must be assessed with complementary measurement approaches.",
